Not exactly. You'd have to keep buying whatever wands he had, in order
to get him to restock. And I imagine he wouldn't do it while you were
in the shop- largely because it'd look rather odd indeed if you were
standing there waiting around and suddenly a new item popped in from
nowhere.

At any rate, what you've got is a method for exploitation based on the
immense patience for extremely tedious things that some nethack
players exhibit. Is this so much worse than pudding farming? I
personally haven't the patience for it, but it seems rather
game-breaking to me. If it truly would be that much worse, you could
program in an explicit "...but no wands of wishing" rule, or maybe
make it so that wands of wishing would be generated with no charges
and one charging already used on them. That's not much worse than a
magic lamp- magic lamps probably being much likelier to exploit...
Because I don't know how the chance of generation of a magic lamp in a
tool shop compares to the chance for a wand of wishing to be generated
in a wand shop, but if I'm not mistaken wand shops are pretty darn
rare to begin with, so you'd have to _find_ one of those. (Wand shop
with 12 squares? Good luck.)

